From: Zixuan Zhang <zixuan.zhang.uj@renesas.com>
Date: Tue, 9 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] video: rzg2l: play a multi-frame intro from a
 self-advancing display list chain

The driver scans out one still image and holds it until Linux takes over.
On this board that is about six seconds of a static picture - the display
comes up at ~1.05 s and the first Linux application does not draw until ~3.6 s
after the boot has been tuned - so almost all of the "boot animation" the
user sees is a photograph.

The VSPD can do better on its own.  A display list header names the header
for the next frame in pNext_header, and flags bit 0 (next_frame_auto_start)
tells the hardware to carry on when the current frame is done.  Point each
header's body at a different frame buffer and it walks an animation with no
CPU involvement whatsoever - through the rest of U-Boot, through the kernel's
boot, right up until something commits a new display list.

This is not a new mechanism.  rzg2l_vcpd_dl_init() already relies on the
hardware following pNext_header from the configuration header to a second one
and then looping it onto itself; that is what keeps the picture up during the
kernel boot today.  All that changes is the number of hops.

  - Frame 0 loads and the pipeline comes up on it exactly as before, so the
    time to first pixel does not move.  Header 0 loops onto itself.
  - The remaining frames load behind a picture that is already on screen.
  - A chain is built for frames 1..N-1, each frame repeated
    round(60 * rate_ms / 1000) times so it is held for rate_ms, and armed with
    a single store to header 0's pNext_header.  The hardware picks that up at
    the next frame boundary: no register write, no glitch.
  - The last header points at itself.  The intro plays once and parks.

Parking matters more than it looks.  The chain keeps advancing after U-Boot
is gone, so neither U-Boot nor the kernel can know which frame is on screen
at an arbitrary later moment.  Ending on a fixed frame removes the question:
a Linux application takes over from a known frame however long the kernel took.

Frame count and rate come from the "logo_frames" and "logo_rate_ms"
environment variables, so retuning the intro needs neither a rebuild nor a
re-flash of fip.  Both are bounded - by the room between LCD_SRCM_ADDR and
the header area for frames, by the header area itself for the chain length,
and by the refresh period for the rate.  A frame that fails to load ends the
intro early rather than aborting it; a short animation is a much better
failure than a dead one.

Cost is one extra ext4load and unzip per frame, about 70 ms each at 1080p,
paid after the display is already lit.

rzg2l_video_update() is kept because cmd/booti.c and common/autoboot.c call
it, but it does nothing now.  It used to step two headers from the CPU, one
call per frame; the chain needs no help and, unlike that, keeps animating
after the hand-over to Linux.  LCD_SRCM_FB_NUM goes with it - the frame count
is a run-time property now.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [RZ/G3L boot animation, board specific]
